Chapter: BackgammonWasm
  cites Games chapter Backgammon
  cites Games chapter Rng
  cites Foreword chapter List

 The browser interface for Backgammon, on the handle contract in
 games-backlog.md GAME-11. Twenty-four signed points plus two bars and two
 trays is far past what the export wrapper's signed i32 could carry, so the
 page holds the board's address.

 The copy here guards the apply path only. Unlike Connect Four, this engine's
 move search does not need one: `bg-pick-loop` scores a candidate by reading
 `list-at`, `pt-blocked` and `all-in-home`, and never applies a move to find
 out what it does, so it cannot corrupt the board it is searching (GAME-13).
 `bg-move-piece` and `bg-enter-from-bar` do write through `list-set-at`, so
 `bg-wasm-step` copies before it applies.

 A turn is dice, not a move. The engine applies ONE die at a time and does
 not change hands, so the page drives a turn the way `bg-game-loop` does:
 step for each die, four times on a double, then end the turn. Splitting it
 that way is what lets a watching page show the checkers move one at a time
 rather than jumping a whole turn at once.

 We say:

Section: Copying

  bg-copy-points : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  bg-copy-points (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 24 then acc
    else bg-copy-points src (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at src i))

  bg-copy-board : BackgammonBoard -> BackgammonBoard
  bg-copy-board (b) =
    BackgammonBoard {
      points = bg-copy-points (b.points) 0 [],
      bar-white = b.bar-white,
      bar-black = b.bar-black,
      off-white = b.off-white,
      off-black = b.off-black,
      current-player = b.current-player
    }

Section: The Browser Interface

  bg-wasm-new : BackgammonBoard
  bg-wasm-new = backgammon-new

  bg-wasm-die : Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-die (seed) = rng-range (rng-new seed) 1 6

  bg-wasm-step : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> BackgammonBoard
  bg-wasm-step (b) (die) =
    if bg-check-win b then b
    else if die < 1 then b
    else if die > 6 then b
    else bg-apply-die (bg-copy-board b) die

  bg-wasm-endturn : BackgammonBoard -> BackgammonBoard
  bg-wasm-endturn (b) =
    if bg-check-win b then b
    else BackgammonBoard {
      points = bg-copy-points (b.points) 0 [],
      bar-white = b.bar-white,
      bar-black = b.bar-black,
      off-white = b.off-white,
      off-black = b.off-black,
      current-player = 1 - b.current-player
    }

Section: One Move At A Time

 `bg-apply-die` picks its own point, which is all a watching page needs and
 nothing a playing one can use. These name the point instead, so a person can
 say which checker moves. The legality test is the same one `bg-pick-loop`
 applies when it searches: `all-in-home` gates bearing off, `pt-blocked` gates
 a landing, and a player with a checker on the bar may do nothing else until
 it is entered.

  bg-wasm-can : BackgammonBoard, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-can (b) (from) (die) =
    if bg-check-win b then 0
    else if die < 1 then 0
    else if die > 6 then 0
    else if from < 0 then 0
    else if from >= 24 then 0
    else let player = b.current-player
    in if on-bar b player then 0
    else let v = list-at (b.points) from
    in let mine = if player == 0 then v > 0 else v < 0
    in if mine == False then 0
    else let target = if player == 0 then from - die else from + die
    in let bearing-off = if player == 0 then target < 0 else target >= 24
    in if bearing-off then (if all-in-home b player then 1 else 0)
    else if target < 0 then 0
    else if target >= 24 then 0
    else if pt-blocked b target player then 0
    else 1

  bg-wasm-move : BackgammonBoard, Integer, Integer -> BackgammonBoard
  bg-wasm-move (b) (from) (die) =
    if bg-wasm-can b from die == 0 then b
    else bg-move-piece (bg-copy-board b) from die

  bg-wasm-can-enter : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-can-enter (b) (die) =
    if bg-check-win b then 0
    else if die < 1 then 0
    else if die > 6 then 0
    else let player = b.current-player
    in if on-bar b player == False then 0
    else let target = if player == 0 then 24 - die else die - 1
    in if pt-blocked b target player then 0
    else 1

  bg-wasm-enter : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> BackgammonBoard
  bg-wasm-enter (b) (die) =
    if bg-wasm-can-enter b die == 0 then b
    else bg-enter-from-bar (bg-copy-board b) die

 A die with no legal move anywhere is forfeit rather than a stuck turn, and
 the page needs to know which case it is in before it offers a click.

  bg-wasm-any : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-any (b) (die) =
    if bg-wasm-can-enter b die == 1 then 1
    else bg-wasm-any-loop b die 0

  bg-wasm-any-loop : BackgammonBoard, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-any-loop (b) (die) (i) =
    if i >= 24 then 0
    else if bg-wasm-can b i die == 1 then 1
    else bg-wasm-any-loop b die (i + 1)

  bg-wasm-point : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-point (b) (i) =
    if i < 0 then 0
    else if i >= 24 then 0
    else list-at (b.points) i

  bg-wasm-bar : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-bar (b) (player) =
    if player == 0 then b.bar-white else b.bar-black

  bg-wasm-off : BackgammonBoard, Integer -> Integer
  bg-wasm-off (b) (player) =
    if player == 0 then b.off-white else b.off-black

  bg-wasm-cur : BackgammonBoard -> Integer
  bg-wasm-cur (b) = b.current-player

  bg-wasm-done : BackgammonBoard -> Integer
  bg-wasm-done (b) = if bg-check-win b then 1 else 0

  bg-wasm-winner : BackgammonBoard -> Integer
  bg-wasm-winner (b) = bg-winner b

